  "content": {
    "id": "p17-q7-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "7",
    "prompt": "For the curve $y=e^{x^2}$ at $(1,e)$, find the equation of the tangent and normal in general form.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "Tangent: $2ex-y-e=0$.\n\nNormal: $x+2ey-2e^2-1=0$.",
      "worked": "By the chain rule,\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx}&=2xe^{x^2}\\\\m_{\\text{tangent}}&=2(1)e^{1^2}\\\\m_{\\text{tangent}}&=2e.\\end{aligned}$$\nTangent:\n$$\\begin{aligned}y-e&=2e(x-1)\\\\2ex-y-e&=0.\\end{aligned}$$\nThe normal gradient is the negative reciprocal of the tangent gradient:\n$$m_{\\text{normal}}=-\\frac{1}{2e}.$$\nNormal:\n$$\\begin{aligned}y-e&=-\\frac{1}{2e}(x-1)\\\\2ey-2e^2&=-x+1\\\\x+2ey-2e^2-1&=0.\\end{aligned}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
